Yes — there is a way to investigate it further, especially because your Pro Plus 2021 looks like a perpetual/lifetime edition.

The important distinction is:

Office 2021 itself is a one-time/perpetual product — it isn't a subscription.

But the particular Pro Plus installation on your laptop is currently only in OOB_GRACE, so we don't yet have evidence of a valid permanent license for it. Microsoft documents OOB_GRACE as a grace state, not the LICENSED state. 

Your ProPlus2021Retail product ID tells us the installed edition/channel, but it doesn't prove that you own the license.


We can check if this Pro Plus has a real license

Run:

cscript "C:\Program Files\Microsoft Office\root\Office16\ospp.vbs" /dstatusall

This is better than /dstatus because it checks all Office licenses installed on the machine.

Look for entries containing:

LICENSE STATUS: ---LICENSED---

If you see another Office Professional Plus 2021 Retail entry with LICENSED, that is very important.

If everything is:

OOB_GRACE

then there is no currently activated permanent Pro Plus license on the machine.

One more thing we can check

In Word go to:

File → Account

Under the Office product name, look for:

> Belongs to: [email address]



Microsoft says the account shown there is the account associated with the Office license. 

